   "source": [
    "# Which Power Projects Actually Get Built?\n",
    "## Development Risk in the Middle East and Beyond\n",
    "\n",
    "*ENSAE Paris, Python for Data Science, 2026-2027*\n",
    "*Sylvain Topeza, Maxime Rolland, Arcadius Kouwagou*\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "**Summary.** Governments and companies announce far more power plants than they build.\n",
    "Using Global Energy Monitor's asset-level data on about 180,000 power units worldwide,\n",
    "enriched with World Bank country data, we measure how much announced capacity is\n",
    "abandoned rather than built, and what explains the difference.\n",
    "\n",
    "Three findings stand out:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **About 40% of the capacity that reached an outcome was abandoned rather than built**\n",
    "   (between 32% and 48% depending on the definition). The rate ranges from 20% for solar\n",
    "   to 57% for coal and 75% for nuclear (this late rate is inflated by old cancellations, \n",
    "   see section 2.4).\n",
    "2. **Development risk depends heavily on the market.** Gulf countries abandon far less\n",
    "   than the rest of the world (14% against 41%), with Saudi Arabia and Qatar close to zero.\n",
    "   The relationship with the quality of regulation is U-shaped: rates are highest both in\n",
    "   fragile countries and in rich, open markets such as the United Kingdom or Australia.\n",
    "   Our results suggest that the way projects are selected matters more than the quality of regulation.\n",
    "3. **The developer matters much less than the market.** Gulf developers abandon none of\n",
    "   their projects at home but 34% abroad, close to their Western and Asian peers (40%).\n",
    "   Controlling for technology, size and country, having a large international sponsor\n",
    "   does not change the odds of abandonment.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Contents**\n",
    "1. Introduction\n",
    "2. Data: collection and cleaning\n",
    "3. A first look: what runs, what is built, what is announced\n",
    "4. How much of what is announced gets built?\n",
    "5. Where is development risk highest?\n",
    "6. Is it the market or the developer?\n",
    "7. Conclusion"
   ]
  },
