   "source": [
    "# 1. Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "### Announced projects and built projects\n",
    "\n",
    "Energy news is full of announcements: a 5 GW solar park here, a new gas plant there,\n",
    "national targets of tens of gigawatts. Many of these projects will never be built.\n",
    "Permits are refused, financing falls through, a tender is cancelled, or a government\n",
    "changes its priorities.\n",
    "\n",
    "For anyone who finances or invests in infrastructure, this gap is central. A developer's\n",
    "value depends on how much of its pipeline will actually reach construction. A government's\n",
    "energy plan is only as credible as its ability to turn announcements into plants. This\n",
    "uncertainty is called **development risk**: the risk that a project dies before it is built.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why the Middle East\n",
    "\n",
    "The Middle East is a striking case for two reasons. First, the Gulf states buy electricity\n",
    "through a single state-owned buyer, which runs tenders and signs long-term purchase\n",
    "contracts with private developers. This model is very different from the open markets of\n",
    "Europe or the United States, where developers propose projects and compete for permits,\n",
    "grid connections and customers. Second, Gulf companies such as ACWA Power (Saudi Arabia) and\n",
    "Masdar (Abu Dhabi) have become major international developers. Following the same companies\n",
    "at home and abroad lets us ask whether development risk comes from the developer or from the\n",
    "market it operates in.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Problem statement\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Which power projects actually get built, and why?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We answer three questions:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **How much of what is announced gets built?** We measure abandonment rates worldwide and\n",
    "   by technology.\n",
    "2. **Where is development risk highest?** We compare countries, with a focus on the\n",
    "   Middle East, and relate the results to country characteristics from the World Bank.\n",
    "3. **Is it the market or the developer?** We compare Gulf developers at home and abroad,\n",
    "   benchmark them against global peers (ENGIE, EDF, Ørsted, Brookfield, etc.) and estimate\n",
    "   a logistic regression that separates technology, country and developer effects."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dc969c15",
