  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cb536811",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.4 Measuring development risk: the outcome sample\n",
    "\n",
    "GEM gives no announcement date, so we cannot follow a cohort of projects from\n",
    "announcement to completion. We use the following approach instead.\n",
    "\n",
    "A project **reached an outcome** if it was either **abandoned** (cancelled or shelved) or\n",
    "**built** (under construction, or in operation since 2010). Projects still in the pipeline\n",
    "have no outcome yet, so they are left out. The **abandonment rate** of a group is then:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\text{abandonment rate} = \\frac{\\text{abandoned MW}}{\\text{abandoned MW} + \\text{built MW}}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Why only plants built since 2010? Including every operating plant, some built in the\n",
    "1950s, would compare decades of successes with the much shorter list of failures that\n",
    "public sources remember, and make the rate artificially low.\n",
    "\n",
    "**This cut-off has a limit that we must state clearly: it applies to built projects only.**\n",
    "Abandoned projects almost never have a date (only 3% do), so all of them enter the\n",
    "calculation, whatever their era. Old cancellations are thus compared with recent\n",
    "constructions. This inflates the rates of technologies with a long history of\n",
    "cancellations: for instance, the 185 GW of abandoned nuclear capacity in the United States\n",
    "largely date back to the 1970s and 1980s. The rates should therefore be read as orders of\n",
    "magnitude, and comparisons between technologies with caution; comparisons between\n",
    "countries and between developers, which mostly concern recent projects, are less affected, \n",
    "except for countries with a long nuclear history (United States, Russia, Japan, United Kingdom). \n",
    "\n",
    "A second choice is optimistic: projects under construction are counted as built, although\n",
    "some will still be abandoned. Section 4 gives the rates without them.\n",
    "\n",
    "Rates are **weighted by capacity**: a 2 GW project weighs a hundred times more than a 20 MW\n",
    "one. This reflects what matters to a grid operator or an investor. Counted in units, the\n",
    "rate would be only 12%, because most units are small solar farms that are rarely abandoned."
   ]
  },
