   "source": [
    "ownership = clean.build_ownership(units)\n",
    "\n",
    "coverage = (\n",
    "    units.assign(has_owner=units[\"owners\"].notna() | units[\"parents\"].notna())\n",
    "    .groupby(\"technology\")\n",
    "    .agg(units=(\"unit_id\", \"count\"), share_with_owner=(\"has_owner\", \"mean\"))\n",
    "    .join(ownership.groupby(\"technology\")[\"share_reported\"].mean()\n",
    "          .rename(\"share_of_owner_records_with_percentages\"))\n",
    "    .sort_values(\"units\", ascending=False)\n",
    ")\n",
    "display(coverage.style.format({\"units\": \"{:,.0f}\", \"share_with_owner\": \"{:.0%}\",\n",
    "                               \"share_of_owner_records_with_percentages\": \"{:.0%}\"}))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "558f9dd8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Owners are known for almost all thermal plants, but for only a quarter of solar units\n",
    "(mostly small projects in Europe and Asia). When owners are listed, their shares are\n",
    "reported in most cases: always for coal and gas, and for about three quarters of solar and\n",
    "wind ownership records. The equal split is therefore used for a minority of records.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2.6 Grouping companies: the sponsor dictionary\n",
    "\n",
    "A company appears under many names: subsidiaries, project companies, spelling variants.\n",
    "To follow **30 large sponsors** (12 from the Gulf, 18 global peers), we wrote a dictionary\n",
    "(`data/sponsors.csv`) with a regular expression per company, and its home country. Two\n",
    "recent acquisitions not yet reflected in GEM are added by hand: Terna Energy (Greece) and\n",
    "Saeta Yield (Spain), both bought by Masdar in 2024.\n",
    "\n",
    "Checking the matches by eye was essential. Our first version contained several false\n",
    "positives: the pattern *Nebras* (Qatar's power investor) also captured the **Nebraska**\n",
    "Public Power District; *Neoen* captured **Neoenergia**, Iberdrola's Brazilian subsidiary;\n",
    "*Mitsui* and *Sumitomo* captured unrelated banks, cement and real-estate companies. Nebras's\n",
    "operating capacity abroad dropped from 5.3 GW to 1.4 GW once American utilities were removed.\n",
    "The table below lists, for each group, the largest raw names it now captures."
   ]
  },
