  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5940cce5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Worldwide, 40% of the capacity that reached an outcome was abandoned rather than built**:\n",
    "about 4,500 GW abandoned against 6,700 GW built. That is roughly 2 GW abandoned,\n",
    "for every 3 GW built"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "id": "ef9a882f",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-21T16:18:49.328242Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-21T16:18:49.328114Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-11-21T16:18:49.747748Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-11-21T16:18:49.746793Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<plotnine.ggplot.ggplot object at 0x7f1417e5db70>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 15,
     "metadata": {
      "image/png": {
       "height": 450,
       "width": 800
      }
     },
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "by_technology = indicators.abandonment_rate(units, \"technology\")\n",
    "plot = plots.plot_rates(\n",
    "    by_technology, \"technology\",\n",
    "    title=\"Nuclear and coal projects are abandoned most often, solar the least\",\n",
    "    subtitle=\"Share of capacity abandoned rather than built, world. Dashed line: all technologies\",\n",
    "    reference=WORLD_RATE,\n",
    ")\n",
    "plots.save(plot, \"rates_by_technology\")\n",
    "plot"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e3a7f562",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The ranking is consistent with the size, complexity and political exposure of each\n",
    "technology. Because abandoned projects are not dated, our data cannot tell *when* they were\n",
    "abandoned; the explanations below are therefore plausible hypotheses, not results.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Nuclear (75%) and coal (57%)** are the riskiest. Nuclear projects are huge, slow and\n",
    "  politically sensitive, and their rate is inflated by old cancellations (see 2.4). Coal\n",
    "  projects were probably abandoned in large numbers as public and private lenders withdrew\n",
    "  their support for coal over the last decade.\n",
    "- **Wind (38%)** is riskier than **solar (20%)**. Wind projects, especially offshore, tend to\n",
    "  face longer permitting, local opposition and, in recent years, cost increases.\n",
    "- **Solar** is the safest technology to develop: projects are modular, quick to build and\n",
    "  rarely contested.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Robustness\n",
    "\n",
    "Two choices can move the indicator in opposite directions. Excluding the statuses that\n",
    "GEM only *infers* (no news for two to four years) gives a **lower bound**. Counting as built\n",
    "only the plants already in operation, since projects under construction can still fail,\n",
    "gives an **upper bound**."
   ]
  },
