  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0e26fc97",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Three patterns emerge.\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. The Gulf stands out, but is not uniform.** Qatar (0%) and Saudi Arabia (3%) build\n",
    "almost everything they announce. In these countries, a state buyer decides how\n",
    "much capacity is needed, runs a tender, and signs a long-term contract with the winner: by\n",
    "the time a project is public, its buyer and its price are already secured. The United Arab\n",
    "Emirates (17%) and Oman (33%) are higher, and Kuwait (46%) is above the world average.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. High rates are not only a sign of fragility.** Myanmar, Zimbabwe or Ukraine have very\n",
    "high rates, as expected. But so do the United Kingdom (64%), Denmark (78%), Sweden (77%) and\n",
    "Australia (79%). In these open markets, many developers propose projects that compete for\n",
    "the same grid connections, subsidies and buyers: most of them will not be built. A high\n",
    "abandonment rate there reflects **who bears the development risk** (private developers,\n",
    "before any contract) rather than a failing state.\n",
    "\n",
    "**3. Measurement matters.** In open markets, projects enter public databases at an early\n",
    "stage (permit applications), while in tender-based markets they often appear only once a\n",
    "developer has been selected. Part of the Gulf's low rate therefore comes from *when*\n",
    "projects become visible. This is a limit of the data, but it is also a feature of the\n",
    "model: in the Gulf, the early, risky stage of development is carried out by the state.\n",
    "\n",
    "What, then, is abandoned in the GCC? The ten largest abandoned projects are shown below."
   ]
  },
