   "source": [
    "countries_wb = countries.merge(wb, on=\"iso3\", how=\"left\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plot = plots.plot_rate_vs_governance(\n",
    "    countries_wb.dropna(subset=[\"regulatory_quality\"]),\n",
    "    title=\"Abandonment is highest at both ends of the regulatory scale\",\n",
    "    subtitle=\"Countries with at least 10 GW of capacity with an outcome; bubble size: capacity\",\n",
    ")\n",
    "plots.save(plot, \"rates_vs_regulation\", 9, 5.5)\n",
    "display(plot)\n",
    "\n",
    "by_income = (\n",
    "    units.loc[units[\"in_outcome_sample\"]]\n",
    "    .merge(wb[[\"iso3\", \"income_group\"]], on=\"iso3\", how=\"left\")\n",
    "    .pipe(indicators.abandonment_rate, \"income_group\")\n",
    ")\n",
    "display(by_income.style.format({\"abandoned_gw\": \"{:,.0f}\", \"built_gw\": \"{:,.0f}\",\n",
    "                                \"outcome_gw\": \"{:,.0f}\", \"n_units\": \"{:,.0f}\",\n",
    "                                \"abandonment_rate\": \"{:.0%}\"}).hide(axis=\"index\"))\n",
    "print(\"Linear correlation between regulatory quality and abandonment rate: \"\n",
    "      f\"{countries_wb[['regulatory_quality', 'abandonment_rate']].corr().iloc[0, 1]:.2f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "by_regulation = (\n",
    "    units.merge(wb[[\"iso3\", \"regulatory_quality\"]], on=\"iso3\", how=\"left\")\n",
    "    .assign(regulation=lambda df: pd.cut(\n",
    "        df[\"regulatory_quality\"], indicators.REGULATION_BINS, labels=indicators.REGULATION_LABELS))\n",
    "    .pipe(indicators.abandonment_rate, \"regulation\")\n",
    ")\n",
    "display(by_regulation[[\"regulation\", \"outcome_gw\", \"n_units\", \"abandonment_rate\"]]\n",
    "        .style.format({\"outcome_gw\": \"{:,.0f}\", \"n_units\": \"{:,.0f}\",\n",
    "                       \"abandonment_rate\": \"{:.0%}\"}).hide(axis=\"index\"))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3d842923",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
        "**The relationship between regulation and development risk is U-shaped.** The linear \n",
    "correlation is close to zero (-0.03 across 73 countries), but this does not mean there is \n",
    "no relationship: a correlation cannot detect a U. The scatter plot and the table by class \n",
    "show it. Countries with weak regulation (Myanmar, Zimbabwe, Libya) abandon 49% of their \n",
    "capacity, and countries with the strongest regulation (Australia, Sweden, Denmark, the \n",
    "United Kingdom) 57%, against 37% in between, where state-led systems such as Saudi Arabia, \n",
    "Qatar or China sit. Section 6 tests this shape in the regression. \n",
    "\n",
    "Income tells a similar story. Upper-middle-income countries have the lowest rate (32%),\n",
    "because this group is dominated by China, where state-owned companies build most of the\n",
    "capacity. High-income countries (46%) abandon more than them, and only slightly less than\n",
    "lower-middle-income (55%) and low-income countries (59%).\n",
    "\n",
        "This is in line with our reading of section 5: the way projects are selected seems to \n",
    "matter more than the quality of regulation. Where a public planner or buyer selects \n",
    "projects before they are announced, few are abandoned; where many developers compete in \n",
    "an open market, many projects die, however good the institutions. \n",
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0dd34a1f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 6. Is it the market or the developer?\n",
    "\n",
    "If the Gulf builds what it announces, is it because its developers are good at executing\n",
    "projects, or because its market model removes most of the risk? To find out, we follow the\n",
    "same companies outside their home country.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 6.1 Developers and acquirers\n",
    "\n",
    "We compute, for each of the 30 sponsors, its portfolio **abroad** by stage, and two ratios:\n",
    "\n",
    "- the **pipeline ratio**: capacity in the pipeline or under construction, divided by\n",
    "  capacity in operation. Above 1, a company mostly develops new projects (a **developer**);\n",
    "  well below 1, it mostly owns plants that already run, often bought from others\n",
    "  (an **acquirer** or operator).\n",
    "- its **abandonment rate** abroad, on its share of each project."
   ]
  },
