  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ba9ee2e6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The chart separates two business models.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Acquirers and operators**, on the left, own mostly operating plants: Brookfield\n",
    "  (pipeline ratio 0.06), Mitsui (0.03), TAQA (0.5). Their abandonment rates are among the\n",
    "  lowest (12% for Brookfield, 7% for TAQA): they buy assets once development risk is gone.\n",
    "- **Developers**, on the right, have pipelines several times larger than their operating\n",
    "  fleet: Masdar (4.2), Scatec (4.6), TotalEnergies (5.0), Ørsted (3.8). They carry the\n",
    "  development risk themselves, and their abandonment rates are higher on average\n",
    "  (61% for Ørsted, 56% for Iberdrola, hit by offshore wind cancellations).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Gulf sponsors sit within these global models rather than apart from them.** Masdar\n",
    "and ACWA Power behave like international renewable developers, with abandonment rates\n",
    "abroad (28% and 37%) close to their peers. TAQA and Nebras look like acquirers.\n",
    "\n",
    "One caveat on Masdar: by attaching Terna Energy and Saeta Yield to it (section 2.6), we also\n",
    "attribute to Masdar the projects these companies abandoned before Masdar bought them.\n",
    "Without them, Masdar's rate abroad falls from 28% to 22%, and that of all Gulf sponsors\n",
    "abroad from 34% to 33%: the conclusions below do not change.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 6.2 The same developers at home and abroad"
   ]
  },
