   "source": [
    "home_abroad = indicators.home_vs_abroad(ownership, by=\"origin\").rename(columns={\"origin\": \"group\"})\n",
    "plot = plots.plot_home_abroad(\n",
    "    home_abroad,\n",
    "    title=\"Gulf developers abandon nothing at home, a third of their projects abroad\",\n",
    "    subtitle=\"Abandonment rate on equity capacity, Gulf sponsors and global peers\",\n",
    ")\n",
    "plots.save(plot, \"home_vs_abroad\", 8, 3.2)\n",
    "display(plot)\n",
    "\n",
    "gulf_detail = (\n",
    "    indicators.home_vs_abroad(ownership, by=\"sponsor_group\")\n",
    "    .loc[lambda df: df[\"sponsor_group\"].isin([\"ACWA Power\", \"Masdar\", \"TAQA\", \"Nebras\"])]\n",
    "    .pivot(index=\"sponsor_group\", columns=\"location\", values=[\"abandonment_rate\", \"outcome_gw\"])\n",
    ")\n",
    "display(gulf_detail.style.format(\"{:.2f}\"))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "24d20a50",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This is the clearest result of the project. **At home, Gulf sponsors abandoned none of the\n",
    "45 GW of capacity that reached an outcome. Abroad, they abandoned 34%**, almost as much as\n",
    "global peers abroad (40%). The same holds company by company: ACWA Power goes from 0% at home\n",
    "to 37% abroad, Masdar from 0% to 28%.\n",
    "\n",
    "Global peers show a much smaller gap between home and abroad (35% and 40%), because their\n",
    "home markets (Europe, Japan, Korea) are themselves open, competitive markets.\n",
    "\n",
    "At home, Gulf developers bid in state\n",
    "tenders: when they win, the project already has a buyer, a price and the government's\n",
    "support. Abroad, they face the same permits, grid constraints and political risks as\n",
    "everyone else. **For an investor, a Gulf developer's near-perfect record at home says little\n",
    "about its projects abroad.**\n",
    "\n",
    "The measurement issue of section 5 applies here too. At home, Gulf sponsors appear in the\n",
    "data only once they have won a tender, so their 0% is partly mechanical. Our data cannot\n",
    "fully separate two explanations that point the same way: the state-tender model itself,\n",
    "and the late stage at which projects become visible in such markets. Either way, the\n",
    "risk that Gulf developers avoid at home is carried by the state, not by them.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 6.3 A logistic regression\n",
    "\n",
    "The comparisons above do not hold everything else equal: Gulf sponsors abroad build mostly\n",
    "solar, in emerging countries, which may have their own risk. We therefore estimate a\n",
    "logistic regression on the units of the outcome sample:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\Pr(\\text{abandoned}_i = 1) = \\Lambda(\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 \\text{technology}_i\n",
    "+ \\beta_2 \\log(\\text{capacity}_i) + \\beta_3 \\text{country}_i\n",
    "+ \\beta_4 \\text{owner}_i)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\Lambda$ is the logistic function. We report **odds ratios**: a ratio of 2 means\n",
    "that the odds of abandonment are twice as high as for the reference category, everything\n",
    "else being equal. Four choices matter:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Weights.** Our descriptive rates are weighted by capacity. Counted in units, the\n",
    "  sample is dominated by small solar farms (two thirds of the units), which are rarely\n",
    "  abandoned. The main models therefore **weight each unit by its capacity**, so that they\n",
    "  explain the same thing as our charts. The unweighted model is shown as a check.\n",
    "- **Owners.** The variable *owner* distinguishes Gulf sponsors abroad, global peers, other\n",
    "  known owners (reference) and **units with no known owner** (58% of the units, mostly\n",
    "  small projects), which form their own category so as not to bias the reference.\n",
    "- **Gulf sponsors at home.** None of their 102 units at home was abandoned. A logit cannot\n",
    "  estimate an effect for a category without a single failure (perfect prediction), so these\n",
    "  units are left out of the regression; their 0% is the descriptive result of 6.2. The\n",
    "  model therefore tests whether Gulf sponsors *abroad* differ from other owners.\n",
    "- **Standard errors** are clustered by country, because projects in the same country share\n",
    "  the same regulations and grid. Note that the GCC effect is then identified by only six\n",
    "  countries, so its p-value should be read with caution. statsmodels also warns that\n",
    "  clustered errors are not fully supported with weights; the unweighted model, where this\n",
    "  issue does not arise, gives the same conclusions on owners.\n",
    "\n",
    "We estimate model A, which describes the host country by its region only (GCC, rest of the\n",
    "Middle East, rest of the world), and model B, which adds the World Bank income group and\n",
    "Regulatory Quality score, with a squared term to capture the U shape seen in section 5."
   ]
  },
