  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bfa9a0fc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The weighted models confirm the descriptive analysis:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Technology** has the largest effect. All else equal, the odds of abandonment are about\n",
    "  6 times higher for nuclear, 3.7 times for coal and 2.1 times for wind than for solar\n",
    "  (model A).\n",
    "- **Size** matters: each doubling of capacity raises the odds of abandonment by about 40%\n",
    "  (odds ratio of 1.6 per unit of log capacity).\n",
    "- **Being in the GCC** divides the odds by about three (odds ratio 0.35 in model A, 0.28 in\n",
    "  model B), all else equal, with the caveat on six clusters noted above.\n",
    "- **We find no significant difference between sponsors**: Gulf sponsors abroad (0.98) and \n", 
    "  global peers (1.13) have odds ratios close to 1 and far from significant, in all three models. Once technology,\n",
    "  size and location are accounted for, projects led by large international companies are\n",
    "  abandoned as often as those of other known owners.\n",
    "- **Large projects with no known owner are abandoned more often** (2.4 in model A). One \n",
    "  hypothesis is that many were announced before any developer was selected. But the causality \n",
    "  may also run the other way: GEM often documents owners once a project is built, so an \n",
    "  abandoned project is more likely to remain without a known owner.\n",
    "\n",
    "Model B adds country characteristics. **The squared regulatory quality term is positive \n",
    "and significant (1.54, p < 0.01)**, and the lowest predicted abandonment is reached at \n",
    "a score of 0.13 (95% confidence interval: -0.46 to 0.73), well within the range of the \n",
    "data (-2.1 to 2.1). This is consistent with the U shape of section 5. The right branch \n",
    "rests on about fifteen open, well-regulated markets, where projects also enter the data \n",
    "at an early stage, which may explain part of the effect. Income groups are not significant \n",
    "once regulation is accounted for. The GCC effect remains: Gulf countries abandon less than \n",
    "their income and regulation would predict, which points again to their procurement model.\n",
    "The rest of the Middle East also has lower odds than the rest of the world (0.52 in model A, \n",
    "0.32 in model B), although this effect also rests on a few countries. In much of the region, power \n",
    "systems are run by state-owned utilities that plan capacity centrally. This suggests that the \n",
    "mechanism is state planning in general, not only the Gulf's tender model. \n",
    "\n",
    "The unweighted model, which mainly describes small units, gives the same message on\n",
    "technology, size, the GCC and sponsors. It differs on owners without a known name, which are\n",
    "mostly small solar farms and rarely abandoned when counted in units.\n",
    "\n",
    "These are associations, not causal effects. Units of the same project are not fully\n",
    "independent (116,296 units belong to about 100,000 projects); clustering by country\n",
    "partly addresses this, and a project-level model would be a natural extension. The \n",
    "standard way to keep Gulf sponsors at home in the model despite perfect prediction \n",
    "would be a penalised (Firth) logistic regression, another natural extension."
   ]
  },
