   "source": [
    "# 7. Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "**Which power projects actually get built, and why?**\n",
    "\n",
    "About 60% of the power capacity that reached an outcome since 2010 was built, and 40% was\n",
    "abandoned. Development risk depends first on the **technology**: solar projects rarely\n",
    "fail, nuclear and coal projects often do. It depends next on the **market**: in the Gulf\n",
    "states, where a single public buyer tenders projects and signs long-term contracts, almost\n",
    "everything that becomes public gets built. In open markets, rich or poor, many projects\n",
    "compete and most of them die before construction. The relationship with the quality of\n",
    "regulation is U-shaped: abandonment is highest both where institutions are weak and in the\n",
    "most open, best-regulated markets. Finally, it depends little on the\n",
    "**developer**: the same Gulf companies that abandon none of their projects at home abandon a\n",
    "third of them abroad, like their Western and Asian peers.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this means for infrastructure investors.**\n",
    "\n",
    "- A pipeline must be valued market by market: a gigawatt announced in Saudi Arabia and a\n",
    "  gigawatt announced in the United Kingdom do not carry the same probability of being built.\n",
    "- A developer's track record in a protected home market does not transfer abroad. Investors\n",
    "  partnering with Gulf developers in third countries take ordinary development risk.\n",
    "- Acquirers such as Brookfield or TAQA avoid most development risk by buying operating\n",
    "  plants: this is the trade-off between core infrastructure, with lower risk, and\n",
    "  value-add strategies, which pay for taking development risk.\n",
    "- In the Middle East, the large renewable pipeline will be credible where it goes through\n",
    "  state tenders; projects announced without a buyer, such as solar farms for green hydrogen,\n",
    "  are the most fragile.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limits.**\n",
    "\n",
    "- The tracker is a snapshot: we cannot follow projects from announcement to completion,\n",
    "  and we approximate outcomes with a 2010 cut-off.\n",
    "- Abandoned projects are not dated, while built projects are restricted to those built\n",
    "  since 2010: old cancellations are compared with recent constructions, which inflates the\n",
    "  rates of technologies such as nuclear. The rates are orders of magnitude, between 32% and\n",
    "  48% worldwide depending on the definition.\n",
    "- Operating units without a start year (10% of units, 189 GW) are left out of the built \n",
    "  projects. Counting them as built would lower the world  rate only slightly, from 40.2% \n",
    "  to 39.5%.\n",
    "- Projects enter the data at different stages depending on the market, which partly\n",
    "  explains the low rates of tender-based markets, and the 0% of Gulf developers at home.\n",
    "- Owners are unknown for many small solar projects, and ownership shares are missing for\n",
    "  about a quarter of renewable ownership records (capacity is then split equally).\n",
    "  Our sponsor dictionary covers 30 companies.\n",
    "- Small installations below GEM's thresholds are not covered.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Extensions.** Comparing two releases of the tracker would allow a true cohort analysis\n",
    "(which projects moved from announced to construction in a year). Adding information on\n",
    "offtake contracts (who buys the electricity) would test directly the role of the\n",
    "single-buyer model.\n",
    "\n",
